{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Scientific theories aim at universal truth, whereas navigation charts are local and pragmatic.","Falsification in science is logical and final for that specific hypothesis, whereas a navigation error is often just a physical obstacle to be circumvented."],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that navigating by the best chart necessarily implies a belief in the stability of the seabed.","If the concept of 'best available route' collapses into 'most probable route' under scrutiny."],"shared_structure":["Selection of a path based on the most recent and severe chart updates rather than a belief that the sea will remain calm.","Acknowledgment that the chosen path may still lead to disaster (unforeseen reef), but it is the most rational choice given current information.","Distinction between predicting safe arrival (induction) and choosing the best available route (critical preference)."],"source_memory_refs":["naval_navigation_strategies"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a captain prefers the updated chart without assuming the ocean won't change, a scientist prefers the corroborated theory without assuming nature is uniform.","The rationality of the action lies in the quality of the selection process, not the certainty of the outcome."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for the better-corroborated theory is defensible on his own terms as a non-inductive methodological rule of 'critical preference' aimed at maximizing truth-content (verisimilitude) rather than predicting future success, though accepting this costs the agent any rational assurance that the chosen theory will not fail in the next instance.","counterconditions":["If preferring a theory for action implicitly assumes it will work in the future, the distinction between risk-minimization and induction collapses.","If Popper's formal definition of verisimilitude is logically untenable (as per Tichý and Miller), the justification for preferring the better-corroborated theory loses its objective basis.","If the 'choice to act' inherently requires a probabilistic assessment of outcomes, then refusing to assign probability renders action irrational within Popper's framework."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument distinguishes between 'inductive inference' (assigning higher probability of truth based on past instances) and 'methodological selection' (choosing the theory that has withstood the most severe tests as the best current candidate for truth). It posits that while we cannot know the future will resemble the past, we can rationally prefer the theory that offers the highest explanatory power and has survived the most rigorous attempts at falsification, treating this preference as a gamble on truth-likeness rather than a calculation of safety.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}